---
title: The Config File
---

All shared frontend settings for the Vonage Video React App live in a single file at the project root: **`app-config.json`**. This is the one place you need to go to control features, display defaults, and other shared frontend behaviour. It uses nested, camelCase JSON.

## Where It Lives

{% filetree %}

- app-config.json   ← All shared frontend configuration goes here
- backend/
  - .env            ← Backend credentials (separate)
- frontend/
- libs/

{% /filetree %}

{% aside %}
Backend credentials (`VONAGE_APP_ID`, `VONAGE_PRIVATE_KEY`) live in `backend/.env`. Local frontend values such as the Codespaces `API_URL` live in `frontend/.env`; this tutorial covers shared frontend configuration in `app-config.json`.
{% /aside %}

## How It Works

`app-config.json` contains nested, camelCase JSON. Run `yarn sync:env` after each edit to generate `env.sh`, which is loaded automatically every time you run the app or trigger a build. Do not edit `env.sh` manually.

```json
{
  "meetingRoomSettings": {
    "allowChat": true,
    "defaultLayoutMode": "activeSpeaker"
  }
}
```

## Opening the File

Open `app-config.json` in your editor:

```sh
code app-config.json
```

The defaults work out of the box — all major features are enabled and the resolution is set to 1280×720. You only need to edit the file when you want to change a specific behaviour.

{% aside type="caution" %}
After editing `app-config.json` you must run `yarn sync:env` and restart the app (`yarn dev`) for the changes to take effect.
{% /aside %}

Now let's look at the feature flags you can control.
